{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310645,"nonce":"KpPGAx3KaajNHAYzat6q1MXDCuQ=","title":"OPEN, MEASURED: a CLASS instance allocated in a loop body is NEVER freed — insert_free.rs skips non-array inner allocs by design. Leak is ...","message":"`/tmp/leak.hx`: a loop calling a non-inline factory that returns `new P(...)`,\nresult assigned to a loop-carried local.\n\n| iterations | peak RSS | over ~96 MB baseline | per alloc |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 300 K | 105.4 MB | ~9 MB | ~31 B |\n| 3 M | 188.0 MB | ~92 MB | ~31 B |\n| 10 M | 403.3 MB | ~307 MB | ~31 B |\n\nPerfectly linear ⇒ nothing is reclaimed. `P` = header + 2 slots = 24 B + malloc\noverhead ≈ 31 B, so it is one retained object per iteration exactly.\n\n\n`a44fc17a` \"fix: SRA/InsertFree correctness\" introduced the entry-block vs\ninner-block partition and, with it, the leak. Its own comment:\n\n    // Entry-block allocs: free at return blocks (original behavior)\n    // Inner-block allocs: skip for now.\n    // The \"last-use block\" approach is unsound for loop-carried allocations:\n    // if an alloc is used in a loop body, freeing at the \"last use\" block frees\n    // it after the first iteration, causing use-after-free on subsequent\n    // iterations. These allocations are typically eliminated by SRA (promoted\n    // to registers). Any remaining inner-block allocs will leak, which is\n    // acceptable.\n\nSo a use-after-free was traded for a leak, resting on \"typically eliminated by\nSRA\". Later commits clawed back part of the skip — `569a0554` for non-escaping\narrays, `1ed90fd8` for received fresh arrays (which also renamed it \"the\nhistorical skip\"), `debcedb3` for strings — leaving class instances the only\ncategory still leaked. Chronology of the arc:\n`a44fc17a` → `2f553710` → `33951d68` → `569a0554` → `1ed90fd8` → `debcedb3` →\n`4161b47e`.\n\nNote the two are NOT symmetric: freeing at return blocks cannot fix a loop that\nallocates N objects and keeps only the last pointer — the other N-1 are\nunreachable by then. Per-iteration release (the rotation) or reuse is the only\nthing that can.\n\n\n`compiler/src/ir/insert_free.rs`, the inner-alloc loop (~line 661):\n\n```rust\n// Non-array inner allocs keep the historical skip — freeing at \"last use\" is\n// unsound for loop-carried allocations, and SRA usually promotes them anyway.\nif !is_string \u0026\u0026 !array_alloc_ids.contains(\u0026alloc_id) {\n    continue;\n}\n```\n\nStrings and arrays get an end-of-block release; **class instances get nothing**.\n\n\"SRA usually promotes them anyway\" is the load-bearing assumption and it fails\nwhenever the allocation crosses a call boundary. In the mandelbrot bench\n`complexAdd`/`complexSquare` are `inline`, but the `new Complex` sits inside\n`createComplex`, which is NOT — so the object escapes by RETURN (never freed in\nthe callee) and at the call site is loop-carried (skipped by the rule above).\nNeither end owns it.\n\n\nAny Haxe loop that allocates a class per iteration leaks.\n\n**MEASURED on x86_64 (NUC, 5.86 GB available), not estimated:** a single\n`rayzor run compiler/benchmarks/src/mandelbrot.hx` COMPLETES — checksum\n112798515, exit 0 — with peak RSS **5.73 GB**. It fits by ~130 MB.\n\nAn earlier \"≈27 GB\" estimate here was wrong: it assumed 1000 inner iterations\nfor every pixel, but most escape early. Do not quote it.\n\nThe benchmark RUNNER is what gets OOM-killed (exit 137), because it drives\nseveral backends in one process and that overhead pushes the same workload past\nthe limit. So the OOM is a consequence of the leak, not a separate bug.\n\n\nWritten as `ir/free_on_reassign.rs`, wired into `InsertFreePass`, then reverted.\n**It compiled, passed the repro's shape analysis, and SIGABRTed 5 tests**\n(DebugChecksum, debug_checksum, mandelbrot_small, test_array_iterator,\nnue_smoke_llama_chat).\n\n**It was a DOUBLE FREE, not a premature one.** SIGABRT out of malloc is the\ndouble-free signature; a use-after-free normally SIGSEGVs. `insert_free` already\ncarries a `has_free` guard (insert_free.rs ~460) that checks whether the value\n*or anything derived from it* is already released — an `IrInstruction::Free`, a\n`free()` call, or an anon_drop. The new pass appended its `Free` at the latch\n**without consulting that guard**, so wherever insert_free had already handled\nthe value, mine was the second release. Any retry MUST run the same check.\n\n**This also narrows the bug.** Those 5 tests were not leaking those objects —\ninsert_free frees them already. The leak is specific to the ROTATION shape (a\nheader phi carrying a fresh object each iteration, as in the repro and\nmandelbrot), not to every class allocation in a loop body.\n\nThe design is still believed right: at the latch of a loop whose header carries\na pointer phi, free the value the next iteration is about to replace. The\nlive-out is always the newest value and the exit path does not run the latch, so\nthe freed one is never the one that leaves.\n\nFour things learned, all of which a retry needs:\n\n1. **The latch value is itself a phi.** `$17 = phi *u8 [bb10: $32]`, so an\n   ownership test that only recognises `Alloc`/malloc dests never fires.\n   Ownership must follow phis and Cast/Copy/SsaBarrier to a fixed point.\n2. **A helper returning `new T(...)` must count as owned.** insert_free's\n   `compute_returns_fresh_arrays` is arrays-only; objects need their own\n   \"returns freshly allocated memory\" set.\n3. **MemCopy is not a pointer escape.** Bytes move; identity does not.\n4. **THE BLOCKER: a constructor call reads as an escape.** The allocation is\n   passed as the receiver of its own `new` — `CallDirect fn2(args:[$1, $11])` —\n   and any \"argument passed to a call\" rule rejects it. The right abstraction is\n   `compute_param_retention` (insert_free.rs:1150), but it is gated behind\n   `RZT_PARAM_RETENTION=1` and is documented as unsound today. **Making param\n   retention sound is the real prerequisite**, not more special cases — the\n   SIGABRTs are what happens when you approximate it.\n\n\nFollowed the pattern `569a0554`/`1ed90fd8` used for arrays and strings: drop the\nkind gate, keep `array_confined_to_block`, emit a plain `Free`. Suite stayed\ngreen (181/0/0 — no double free this time, because `allocs_needing_free` already\nexcludes escaping and already-freed allocs). **But it changes nothing**, on any\nshape, measured:\n\n| shape | HEAD | with change |\n|---|---|---|\n| direct `new` in the loop body | flat 95.6 MB | flat 96.2 MB |\n| allocated via a helper, dies each iteration | 110.8 → 233.9 MB | 111.3 → 233.7 MB |\n| rotation (loop-carried) | 188 → 402 MB | unchanged |\n\nWhy: the confinement rule can only fire when the allocation is IN the loop body,\nblock-confined and non-escaping — and **SRA already promotes exactly that case\naway**, which is what the original comment claimed. Where the allocation crosses\na call boundary (the helper shape) it is not an inner alloc of this function at\nall, so the rule never sees it. Do not re-try this; the gate is not what blocks\nthe reclaim.\n\n**MEASUREMENT TRAP:** peak RSS of the mandelbrot benchmark swings 1475–2982 MB\nrun to run — useless for A/B. Use a linear-growth repro (`/tmp/direct.hx`,\n`/tmp/confined.hx`, `/tmp/leak.hx` shapes) where the numbers are stable to\n±0.5 MB. And `git stash push -- \u003cpath\u003e` fails on this box's git; copy the file\naside instead, or the \"baseline\" build silently still contains the change.\n\n\n`find_rotation_releases` in insert_free: a header phi whose incomings are all\nfresh allocations gets `Free(phi.dest)` at the latch. The phi result is the\nPREVIOUS iteration's object, dead by the time the latch runs, so one free pairs\nwith each iteration's alloc; the loop-exit value is selected by a later re-entry\nof the header and is never the one released.\n\nMEASURED, rotation repro (`v = new P(v.x+1.0, v.y+2.0)` in a while loop):\n\n| iterations | before | after (Mac) | after (NUC x86) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 3 M | 162.9 MB | 70.9 MB | 86.3 MB |\n| 10 M | 376.8 MB | 70.8 MB | 86.2 MB |\n\nLinear -\u003e FLAT, results identical. Suite 181/0/0 on both the release and the\npipeline change.\n\n**Two things made it work, both non-obvious:**\n\n1. `ignore_phi` on `pointer_escapes_ex` (mirrors the existing `ignore_returns`).\n   A rotation phi merges two DIFFERENT allocations, so the general phi rule\n   (`all_internal`) rejects each of them — that is why attempt 3 was inert at\n   first, and it is NOT param retention, which changes nothing here.\n2. **A duplicate-release guard.** The pass runs SEVERAL times per module (four,\n   measured, with `RZT_DBG_ROT=1`). Without the guard the second run appends a\n   second `Free` of the same pointer and the program SIGABRTs — exit 134, the\n   identical signature to attempt 1. Every release rule in this pass needs its\n   own already-released check; `has_free` only covers the paths that consult it.\n\nAlso scheduled InsertFree a second time AFTER inlining at each level (it only\never ran before it, where a `new` behind a helper call reads as an escape at\nboth ends and nothing owns it).\n\nProbes: `RZT_DBG_ROT=1` prints `[rot]` for each release and `[rot-rej]` with\nthe guard that rejected a candidate.\n\n\n`pointer_escapes` answers \"does this leave the FUNCTION\". That was sufficient\nwhile every release sat at a return block (after all uses); a release INSIDE the\nloop needs \"is it DEAD\", which is strictly stronger. So `var keep = v` before the\nloop, read after it, had its object released on iteration one — CONFIRMED by\nrunning it: the release fired and `keep` named freed memory. It printed the right\nanswer anyway, because malloc had not recycled the block yet. That is the\ndangerous kind: silent now, corrupt under another allocator.\n\nTwo details the fix turns on, both cost a wrong attempt first:\n- **Follow aliases WITHOUT crossing a phi.** `var b = a` lowers to a Copy with a\n  fresh id, so a raw-id check misses it; but the ordinary `build_derived_set`\n  runs THROUGH the phi and swallows the carried value, rejecting every candidate.\n  `build_alias_set_no_phi` is the middle ground.\n- **Reads BEFORE the loop are initialization.** A constructor's field stores sit\n  in their own preheader blocks (CFG measured: `0 -\u003e 6 -\u003e 7 -\u003e header`), NOT in\n  the block holding the malloc — so \"allow the defining block\" is too narrow and\n  kills the optimisation. Forbid only what the loop EXITS into.\n\nRegression test: `compiler/tests/haxe/test_rotation_alias_live_out.hx`.\n\n\nFour commits, all validated on the DETERMINISTIC instrument\n([[reference_macos_peak_footprint_is_the_metric]]) and `leaks`:\n\n- `ac5eae18` per-iteration temporaries released at the latch. The confined rule\n  only fired for values confined to ONE BLOCK, and a loop body with a\n  short-circuit condition spans several, so the temporary feeding `complexAdd`\n  was never reclaimed. Release is chosen BY KIND — an array still goes through\n  `haxe_array_free` for its data buffer, a class instance gets a plain `Free`.\n- `00645445` a pre-loop block is not an after-loop read. Around an OUTER loop\n  the blocks BEFORE the inner loop are reachable from its exit, so the\n  allocation's own header/zero-fill stores read as a use of a released object.\n  A block that DOMINATES the header runs before the loop — excluded.\n- `f3b46e3f` phi-SRA promoted only the FIRST candidate per invocation, on the\n  assumption the optimizer loop would re-run it. The pass manager runs it once\n  per level, so every other phi-carried allocation stayed on the heap.\n- `a720d615` rebuilt the `.rzb` bundles.\n\n`leaks` on the scaled run: 7,837,196 leaks / 252 MB -\u003e 36,080 / 1.7 MB, and the\nremainder is the image+palette contents, not per-iteration churn.\n\n**ATTRIBUTION METHOD THAT WORKED (reuse it):** `MallocStackLogging=1 leaks\n--atExit -- \u003ccmd\u003e` plus `RAYZOR_DUMP_JIT_MAP=1` (writes\n`/tmp/rayzor_jit_symbols.csv`, address ranges -\u003e qname). Resolve the `???`\nframes against that CSV and the leaking Haxe function is named outright — here\nevery stack was `benchmarks.Mandelbrot.new`. Use `--preset script` so one\nbackend's symbols cover the whole program; the default tiered preset leaves the\nhot code in an unmapped region. Scale the workload down first (SIZE=5) or stack\nlogging over 250M allocations is unusable.\n\n\nMandelbrot wall clock 2.98s -\u003e 4.3-4.7s. Expected: ~250M `free` calls were\nadded to the hot loop. The right answer is to stop ALLOCATING, not to free\nfaster — and measured, neither iterating phi-SRA nor marking\n`createComplex`/`complexLength2` inline eliminates those allocations or\nrecovers the time.\n\n\nSame MIR, mandelbrot execute: rayzor-gcc 215 ms, rayzor-llvm 1966 ms,\nrayzor-tiered 1963 ms, rayzor-cranelift 2460 ms. Cranelift is WORSE than\ntiered, and LLVM lands exactly where tiered lands. NOT explained by allocation\nelimination (tested) and NOT explained by arithmetic (see below). This is the\nthing to chase.\n\n\niterSum on the identical program: haxe --interp 97,931,565 · haxe-jvm\n97,931,565 · haxe-cpp 97,811,109 · rayzor 97,810,722. TWO CLUSTERS: {jvm,\ninterp} vs {cpp, rayzor}. rayzor sits with the production C++ target, 387 apart\nin 97.8M (0.0004%), while the jvm/interp cluster is 0.12% away. Consistent with\nFMA contraction, which C/C++ and LLVM/Cranelift do by default and the JVM\nforbids. The palette sub-sum is IDENTICAL everywhere, so `Std.int`/`Math.abs`\nare fine. **Do NOT treat `haxe --interp` as the reference — it is an outlier\nand nobody ships on it.**\n\n\n- **There is NO heap corruption.** A `double free or corruption (out)` seen\n  under an LD_PRELOAD malloc counter was the SHIM's fault: `MALLOC_CHECK_=3`\n  with no preload is clean, exit 0, correct checksum. Do not chase it.\n- **Inlining is NOT the blocker.** Marking `createComplex` inline, then\n  `complexLength2` too (4 inline fns), left the leak unchanged at 6.21-6.23 GB.\n  The allocation being local and non-escaping is not sufficient — nothing\n  reclaims a loop-carried object and SRA does not promote through the phi.\n\n\nFreeing at last use IS unsound for a loop-carried value, so the rule is not\nsimply wrong — but the principled answer is **free-on-reassign**: when an owned,\nnon-escaped pointer local is overwritten, release the previous value first. That\nneeds the same escape test already in this pass, plus proof the old value is not\naliased. The alternative already half-present is LICM + `ir/escape_analysis.rs`\nhoisting a non-escaping alloc to the preheader and reusing one buffer across\niterations — that works only when the allocation does not escape the iteration,\nwhich is false here because it is returned.\n\nRelated: [[reference_insert_free_actual_conditions.md]] documents the same skip\nfrom the other direction. [[perf_peak_memory_levers_measured]] for the wider\nmemory arc.\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): MANDELBROT LEAK FIXED 5.9→0.05 GB, ~45% slower. OPEN: gcc 9x faster than our LLVM JIT on the same MIR. PREREQ = sound `compute_param_retention`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_loop_body_class_alloc_never_freed` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}